\chapter{\code{ASTNode}: The Value-Type Tree}
\label{ch:astnode}

\section{The problem: a tree that every part of the toolkit can share}

A domain-specific language needs a common representation for the trees its
front ends produce. A parser must build one; a pretty-printer must read one;
a rewriter must transform one; an evaluator must walk one. If each of those
components invents its own node type, the components cannot be mixed, and
the author of the DSL ends up writing the same tree five times.

\DSLtk{} answers this with a single type: \api{dsl::ASTNode}, defined at
line 6525 of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}. It is deliberately small. It has no
virtual functions, no type tags in the C++ sense, no reference-counted
handles, and no inheritance. It is a \emph{value type}: copying an
\api{ASTNode} copies an entire subtree, and two nodes that print the same
way compare equal. Everything else in the AST subsystem --- the factories
of \chapref{ch:leaf-and-node}, the traversal idioms of \chapref{ch:traversal},
the rules of \chapref{ch:rewrite}, and the fixpoint engine of
\chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} --- is built on top of this one class.

That decision has consequences, and the chapter is largely about them. The
storage is not a \code{std::variant}; the accessors are not iterators; the
comparison operator has no inequality companion; and the copy semantics are
the dominant cost in every algorithm that touches the tree. A reader who
understands those four facts will not be surprised by anything later in
Part Five.

It is worth saying what the alternative designs would have cost. A
pointer-based tree, in which each node is separately allocated and the
children are held through pointers, avoids the copy cost entirely, but it
introduces ownership questions that \DSLtk{} declines to answer: who frees
a node, who shares it, and what happens when two parts of a front end want
to share a subtree. A reference-counted tree answers the first two
questions and adds a third --- the cycle problem --- that has no pleasant
solution in a header-only library. A \code{std::variant}-based tree
expresses the leaf/inner distinction in the type system, but it forces the
user to name both shapes at every use site and makes the recursive
definition awkward to write, because a \code{variant} cannot contain
itself.

\api{ASTNode} takes the third path. It stores a discriminator and the
union of the two shapes side by side, it owns its whole subtree, and it
copies eagerly. The price is the copy cost described in
\secref{sec:astnode-copy}; the benefit is that an \api{ASTNode} can be
stored in a container, returned from a function, compared, and thrown
around without any of the ceremony that a raw-pointer tree demands. For a
toolkit whose job is to be a building block rather than a complete
product, that is the right trade.

\section{A worked example}

The program below builds a small expression tree, prints it, compares it
structurally, and demonstrates that the copy is deep. It is complete and
compiles against the shipped header.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Building and inspecting an \api{ASTNode} tree.},label={lst:astnode-demo}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

int main ()
{
  using dsl::ASTNode;

  // 1. The default node: empty tag, leaf, empty value.
  ASTNode empty;
  std::cout << "default  : " << empty.dump () << '\n';

  // 2. A leaf carries a tag and a textual value.
  ASTNode one = dsl::leaf<"num"> (1);
  std::cout << "leaf     : " << one.dump () << '\n';

  // 3. An inner node carries a tag and an ordered list of children.
  ASTNode sum = dsl::node<"add"> (one, dsl::leaf<"num"> (2));
  std::cout << "inner    : " << sum.dump () << '\n';

  // 4. Structural equality.
  ASTNode sum2 = dsl::node<"add"> (dsl::leaf<"num"> (1), dsl::leaf<"num"> (2));
  std::cout << "equal    : " << std::boolalpha << (sum == sum2) << '\n';

  // 5. The tree is a deep copy: mutating the copy does not affect the original.
  ASTNode mutable_copy = sum;
  mutable_copy.children ()[0] = dsl::leaf<"num"> (999);
  std::cout << "copy     : " << mutable_copy.dump () << '\n';
  std::cout << "original : " << sum.dump () << '\n';

  // 6. child() is bounds checked.
  try
    {
      (void) one.child (0);
    }
  catch (const std::out_of_range &)
    {
      std::cout << "child(0) : threw std::out_of_range\n";
    }
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:astnode-demo}.},label={lst:astnode-demo-out}]
default  : ()
leaf     : (num 1)
inner    : (add (num 1) (num 2))
equal    : true
copy     : (add (num 999) (num 2))
original : (add (num 1) (num 2))
child(0) : threw std::out_of_range
\end{lstlisting}

Three observations are worth pausing on. The default-constructed node
prints as \code{()}: it has an empty tag and is a leaf with an empty value, so
\api{dump()} emits an open parenthesis, the (empty) tag, and a close
parenthesis. The copy in step~5 shares nothing with the original: replacing
\api{children()[0]} of the copy leaves the original's first operand at
\code{(num 1)}. And \api{child(0)} on a leaf throws rather than returning a
dangling reference.

\section{The data model}

\api{ASTNode} is a class with four private data members and no base classes.
The layout is shown in \figref{fig:astnode-layout}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
