  \node[dslsmall, below=6mm of self, xshift=-27mm] (tag)
    {\code{std::string}\\ \code{tag_}};
  \node[dslsmall, below=6mm of self] (value)
    {\code{std::string}\\ \code{value_}};
  \node[dslsmall, below=6mm of self, xshift=33mm] (children)
    {\code{std::vector}\\ \code{<ASTNode>}\\ \code{children_}};
  \node[dslsmall, below=9mm of value, xshift=-7mm] (leaf)
    {\code{bool}\\ \code{is_leaf_ = true}};

  \draw[dsledge] (self) -- (tag);
  \draw[dsledge] (self) -- (value);
  \draw[dsledge] (self) -- (children);
  \draw[dsledge] (self) -- (leaf);

  \node[dslterm, below=4mm of children] (kid)
    {ASTNode (child 0)};
  \node[dslterm, below=4mm of kid] (kid2)
    {ASTNode (child 1)};
  \draw[dsledgedash] (children) -- (kid);
  \node[dslsmall, below=4mm of kid2] {recursive by value};

  \node[dslterm, left=4mm of leaf] (disc)
    {discriminator};
  \draw[dslfeedback] (disc) -- (leaf);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{The object layout of \api{ASTNode}: four parallel members, one of
which is a recursive container of values rather than pointers. The
\texttt{is\_leaf\_} flag is a discriminator, not a variant index.}
\label{fig:astnode-layout}
\end{figure}

The members are declared at lines 6645--6648, immediately after the private
helper \api{dump_impl}:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{4.3cm}C{1.0cm}L{7.9cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Member} & \textbf{Line} & \textbf{Semantics} \\
\midrule
\code{std::string tag_}
  & 6645
  & The node's label. Always present, possibly empty. Copied from the
    compile-time \api{FixedString} at construction time. \\
\code{std::string value_}
  & 6646
  & The textual payload of a leaf. Empty for every node built through the
    inner-node constructor. \\
\code{std::vector<}\newline \code{<ASTNode> children_}
  & 6647
  & Ordered sub-nodes, stored \emph{by value}. Empty for a well-formed
    leaf. \\
\code{bool is_leaf_ = true}
  & 6648
  & Discriminator. \code{true} means the node is a leaf and \code{value_}
    is meaningful; \code{false} means the node is an inner node and
    \code{children_} is meaningful. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The four private members of \api{ASTNode}. There is no
\code{std::variant}, no base class, and no pointer to shared storage.}
\label{tab:astnode-members}
\end{table}

The class is best understood as a tagged pair of payloads that happen to be
stored side by side rather than in a variant. The \code{is_leaf_} flag does
not select between two \emph{storages}; it selects between two
\emph{interpretations}. In the leaf interpretation, \code{value_} is the
payload and \code{children_} is ignored; in the inner interpretation,
\code{children_} is the payload and \code{value_} is ignored. Every
accessor in the class follows that convention, and so does
\api{operator==}. A reader coming from a sum-type design should note that
the convention is not enforced anywhere: the flag is a plain
\code{bool} member, and the class offers no way to ask whether the
members agree with it.

The crucial point in \tabref{tab:astnode-members} is the third row.
\code{children_} is a \code{std::vector<ASTNode>}, not a
\code{std::vector<ASTNode*>} and not a vector of variants. Every child is
stored inline. That single decision determines the copy semantics, the
memory profile, and the shape of every algorithm in the subsystem.

\subsection{Construction}

There are exactly three constructors, and they partition the node space
cleanly.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{5.4cm}C{1.0cm}L{6.8cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Signature} & \textbf{Line} & \textbf{Result} \\
\midrule
\code{ASTNode()}
  & 6528
  & Empty tag, leaf, empty value, no children. Prints as \code{()}. \\
\code{ASTNode(std::string_view tag,}\newline
\code{std::string value)}
  & 6531
  & A leaf: the tag is copied, the value is moved in, \code{is_leaf_} is
    \code{true}. \\
\code{ASTNode(std::string_view tag,}\newline
\code{std::vector<ASTNode> children)}
  & 6537
  & An inner node: the tag is copied, the children are moved in,
    \code{is_leaf_} is \code{false}. The value stays empty. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The three constructors of \api{ASTNode}. The two-argument overloads
are distinguished by the type of the second argument, so there is no
ambiguity between them.}
\label{tab:astnode-ctors}
\end{table}

The two two-argument overloads are not ambiguous because
\code{std::string} and \code{std::vector<ASTNode>} are unrelated types: no
implicit conversion exists in either direction. A caller who wants a leaf
must pass something convertible to \code{std::string}; a caller who wants an
inner node must pass a vector. Note also that neither two-argument
constructor sets \code{value_} for an inner node, and neither sets
\code{children_} for a leaf. The two payload members are mutually
exclusive by construction.

