\code{operator<<}.}
\label{tab:astnode-api}
\end{table}

Three absences deserve emphasis, because code written against another tree
library will not compile against this one.

\begin{itemize}
\item There is no \code{child_at}. The method is called \code{child}, and it
  has both a const and a non-const overload.
\item There are no iterators. \code{children()} returns a reference to the
  underlying \code{std::vector}, so a caller who wants iteration writes
  \code{for (auto const& child : node.children())} exactly as the header
  itself does at lines 6860, 9339 and 9997. There is no \code{begin()}, no
  \code{end()}, and no range-for friendliness of its own.
\item There is no \code{operator<<}. A node does not stream; you must call
  \api{dump()} and stream the resulting string.
\end{itemize}

There is also no mutable accessor for \code{value_} and no mutable accessor
for \code{tag_}. This is easy to miss and it constrains what an in-place
rewriter can do: it can change a node's \emph{children}, but it cannot
change a leaf's text or any node's tag without rebuilding the node. The
rewrite engine of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} sidesteps this by never
mutating in place at all.

\subsection{Dumping}
\label{sec:astnode-dump}

\api{dump()} (line 6606) is the debugging primitive of the whole subsystem.
It allocates an output stream, delegates to the private recursive helper
\api{dump_impl} (line 6625), and returns the resulting string. The stream
is a \code{std::ostringstream}.

\begin{pseudocode}[S-expression rendering of a node]
\textbf{function} dump\_impl (n, os)\\
\quad os \code{<-} '(' \code{<-} n.tag\\
\quad \textbf{if} n.is\_leaf \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} n.value is not empty \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\quad os \code{<-} ' ' \code{<-} n.value\\
\quad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \textbf{for} c \textbf{in} n.children \textbf{do}\\
\qquad\quad os \code{<-} ' '\\
\qquad\quad dump\_impl (c, os)\\
\quad os \code{<-} ')'
\end{pseudocode}

The rendering is therefore: a leaf with a non-empty value becomes
\code{(tag value)}; a leaf with an empty value becomes \code{(tag)}; an inner
node becomes \code{(tag child1 child2 ...)}. The example in the header's own
comment at line 6603 is exact: a node tagged \code{"add"} with two numeric
leaves dumps as \code{"(add (num 1) (num 2))"}.

To see the rendering in detail, consider the tree
\code{node<"add">(leaf<"num">(1), leaf<"num">(2))}. The outer node is not
a leaf, so \api{dump_impl} writes an open parenthesis, the tag
\code{"add"}, and then, for each child in order, a space followed by the
child's own rendering. The first child is a leaf whose value is non-empty,
so it writes \code{"(num 1)"}; the second writes \code{"(num 2)"}. The
outer node then writes its closing parenthesis, producing
\code{"(add (num 1) (num 2))"}. Notice that the space separator is written
by the parent, not by the child: a child never knows whether it is the
first, the last, or the only child, and the format therefore does not
depend on the number of siblings.

The output is an S-expression in the informal sense, but it is \emph{not} the
\api{SExpr} of \chapref{ch:sexpressions} and it is not round-trippable
through \api{parse_sexpr}. No quoting is applied, so a leaf value that
contains a space or a parenthesis is indistinguishable from a differently
shaped tree. \code{leaf<"str">("hello world")} dumps as
\code{(str hello world)}, which reads as a three-element list rather than a
two-element one. Use \api{ast_to_sexpr} (line 9332) when a lossless
representation matters.

\subsection{Equality}
\label{sec:astnode-equality}

\api{operator==} at line 6614 is the whole of the comparison surface. It
proceeds in three steps: the tags must be equal, the leaf-ness must be
equal, and then either the values are compared (for two leaves) or the two
children vectors are compared (for two inner nodes).

\begin{pseudocode}[Structural comparison of two nodes]
\textbf{function} equals (a, b)\\
\quad \textbf{if} a.tag \code{!=} b.tag \textbf{then return} false\\
\quad \textbf{if} a.is\_leaf \code{!=} b.is\_leaf \textbf{then return} false\\
\quad \textbf{if} a.is\_leaf \textbf{then return} a.value = b.value\\
\quad \textbf{return} a.children = b.children
\end{pseudocode}

Two consequences follow. First, the comparison is a \emph{structural} one:
two trees built independently but identically are equal, which is what makes
\api{ASTNode} usable as the value type of a hash-consed or memoised
computation. Second, the comparison is asymmetric in what it looks at: for
an inner node the \code{value_} member is never consulted. In practice
this is unobservable, because the inner-node constructor leaves
\code{value_} empty and nothing in the public API can fill it in --- but it
does mean that equality is not simply "all members are equal".

There is no explicitly declared \code{operator!=}, but C++20's rewritten
comparison candidates make \code{a != b} work using the Boolean equality
operator. There is also no
\code{operator<}, no hash function, and no \code{std::hash} specialisation,
so an \api{ASTNode} cannot be a key in an \code{unordered_container} and
cannot be sorted.

\section{The cost of a copy}
\label{sec:astnode-copy}

Because \code{children_} holds values, the implicitly generated copy
constructor of \api{ASTNode} copies the entire subtree beneath it. There is
no sharing, no reference count, and no copy-on-write. The cost model is
therefore:

\begin{pseudocode}[Cost of copying a node]
\textbf{function} copy\_cost (n)\\
\quad \textbf{return} $1 + \sum_{c \in \mathrm{children}(n)} \mathrm{copyCost}(c)$
\end{pseudocode}

For a tree of \(N\) nodes this is \(\Theta(N)\) node copies and, in the
worst case, \(\Theta(N)\) string copies as well, because each copied node
duplicates its own tag and (for leaves) its value.

\begin{notebox}[The copy is the unit of currency]
Every algorithm in this part of the book pays for trees in copies. The
rewrite engine of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} rebuilds a node for every child
it rewrites; a pass over an \(N\)-node tree allocates on the order of \(N\)
nodes. A reader who is optimising a DSL built on \DSLtk{} should think of
\api{ASTNode} as an immutable-by-convention value with an unusually
expensive copy constructor, and should prefer algorithms that touch each
node a constant number of times.
\end{notebox}

This is the reason the rewrite engine is written as a copy-based fold rather
than as an in-place mutation, and it is the reason \chapref{ch:traversal}
devotes attention to the difference between the two styles.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\paragraph{No variant, no sum type.}
The storage is four parallel members, not a \code{std::variant} over a leaf
shape and an inner shape. Two consequences follow. The size of an
\api{ASTNode} is the size of the \emph{union} of both shapes plus the
discriminator, so every node pays for a vector and two strings even when it
is a leaf. And the class cannot be extended with a third node kind without
changing the class itself.

\paragraph{The invariants are conventions, not types.}
A well-formed leaf has an empty \code{children_} and a non-empty
\code{value_}; a well-formed inner node has children and an unused
\code{value_}. Nothing enforces this. In particular, the mutable
\code{children()} accessor lets a caller push children into a leaf:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The mutable \api{children()} accessor can break the leaf invariant.},label={lst:astnode-invariant}]
ASTNode leaf ("num", "1");
leaf.children ().push_back (ASTNode ("x", "y"));
std::cout << leaf.dump () << '\n';    // (num 1) -- children are ignored
std::cout << leaf.is_leaf () << '\n'; // 1
std::cout << leaf.size () << '\n';    // 1
\end{lstlisting}

The node then reports \code{is_leaf() == true} and \code{size() == 1}
simultaneously, and \api{dump()} prints it as a leaf, silently discarding
the children. Such a tree violates the application's leaf convention.
The mutation itself is defined C++; consumers may reject it or disagree
about whether the hidden children participate in traversal and equality.

\paragraph{An empty inner node and an empty leaf are different.}
\code{ASTNode("f", std::vector<ASTNode>{})} is an inner node with no
children, whereas \code{ASTNode("f", "")} is a leaf with an empty value.
Both dump as \code{(f)}, but they are not equal, because
\code{is_leaf_} differs. Code that reconstructs trees from \api{dump()}
output cannot distinguish them.

\paragraph{Out-of-range access throws.}
\code{child(i)} uses \code{children_.at(i)}, which throws
\code{std::out_of_range}. The choice is deliberate: the alternative,
\code{operator[]}, would be silent. A rule predicate that assumes a node has
at least one child must therefore check \code{size()} first, and a
predicate that forgets will terminate the whole rewrite with an exception
rather than with a wrong answer. The header's own example at line 6762
gets this right for the first check and relies on the user's discipline for
the rest.

\paragraph{No support for shared structure.}
Because a subtree is copied by value, a DAG cannot be represented: two
parents that both refer to the same child will hold two distinct copies.
Any algorithm that would like to share a subtree --- a common-subexpression
cache, a DAG-shaped term, a cyclic graph --- is outside the model.

\paragraph{No ownership or lifetime machinery.}
There are no smart pointers, no arenas, and no node pools. A tree owns its
entire subtree and frees it on destruction. For very large trees this
means one large recursive destruction, which is exactly the stack-depth
hazard discussed in \chapref{ch:traversal}.

\paragraph{No streaming.}
\code{std::cout << node} does not compile. Use \code{node.dump()}. The
type also has no supplied \code{std::formatter} specialization, so
\code{std::format} requires a conversion or a user formatter. Stream insertion
and formatting customization are separate protocols.

\paragraph{No way to enumerate the tags in use.}
Nothing in the class reports which tags a tree contains, how many distinct
tags it uses, or whether two subtrees agree on their vocabulary. The
\api{ASTVisitor} of \chapref{ch:visitors} performs exactly this
computation --- it walks a schema tree collecting distinct tags --- but it
does so privately, for the purpose of validating that a handler exists for
every tag. A user who wants to know the vocabulary of a tree at run time
must write the walk.

\paragraph{No serialisation format.}
\api{dump()} is a debugging rendering, not a wire format: it does not quote,
it does not distinguish an empty inner node from an empty leaf, and it does
not carry the leaf/inner distinction faithfully through a parse. A user who
needs to persist a tree should either define a grammar on top of
\api{dump()} that it fully controls, or use the \api{SExpr} type of
\chapref{ch:sexpressions}, which has an explicit quoting rule and an
explicit \code{is_atom} discriminator.

\paragraph{No concurrency guarantees, but no hazards either.}
The class says nothing about thread safety, and it needs to say nothing:
because every node owns its subtree outright and there are no shared
mutable subobjects, two threads may read the same tree concurrently
without synchronisation. Two threads may not, however, mutate the same tree
concurrently, because the mutable \code{children()} accessor hands out a
reference into the node's internals. As with any standard container, the
usual rule applies: concurrent readers are fine, and any writer must
exclude every reader.

\paragraph{The \code{size()} / \code{children()} relationship.}
\code{size()} is defined as \code{children_.size()} and nothing else. It
is not a count of the node's own constituents, and it is not a count of
descendants. A rule that wants to know how many leaves a subtree contains
must count them; there is no cached statistic on the node to consult. This
keeps the class cheap --- the node has no derived state --- at the price of
making every global query a full traversal.

\section{Closing}

\api{ASTNode} is a small class with large consequences. Its four parallel
members give it a fixed, predictable layout; its value semantics make
trees safe to copy and easy to compare; and its recursive-by-value child
storage makes copying the dominant operation in every algorithm built on
top of it. Its interface is deliberately minimal --- no variant, no
iterators, no inequality operator, no streaming --- which means that the
idioms that fill those gaps are user-written. \chapref{ch:leaf-and-node}
supplies the construction idioms; \chapref{ch:traversal} supplies the
walking idioms; \chapref{ch:rewrite} and \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} supply
the transformation idioms.
